{"episodes":{"185241":"Showers with embedded thunderstorms developed across central and southern New Mexico ahead of an upper-level low pressure system over northern Mexico. Outflow winds ahead of this activity produced a strong wind gust of 58 mph at the Stallion Runway mesonet on the White Sands Missile Range grounds.","185697":"Thunderstorms developed near a warm front across central and east-central Wisconsin during the mid to late morning hours on Tuesday (10/24).  The storms lifted north across north-central and northeast Wisconsin during the early afternoon hours.  The strongest storms produced large hail up to 3 inches in diameter.","185939":"Thunderstorms developed and moved rapidly north across southeast Arizona during the early afternoon. Large hail fell in parts of the Tucson Metro.","186290":"A corridor of heavy rainfall, centered from southwest into north-central Oklahoma, occurred from the morning into early evening of the 24th. Widespread one to nearly four-inch rainfall totals were observed/reported during the period, with the highest totals located across far northern Oklahoma (Kay County). This resulted in sporadic occurrences of (flash) flooding.","186463":"A late season severe weather event brought large hail across southern and central Minnesota. Storms developed during the late morning, and strengthened as they moved across southern Minnesota. There were numerous reports of severe hail, with a few reports of hail in excess of 2 inches in Brown and Redwood Counties.","186527":"The combination of abundant moisture and strong thunderstorms produced some flash flooding across portions of Kimble County.","186590":"A digging trough across northern Mexico produced sufficient instability and wind shear for scattered supercells across portions of far west Texas and southeast New Mexico. Large hail was observed with a few of these thunderstorms.","186665":"A series of storms moved through southeast Minnesota during the early morning hours of October 24th. Scattered storms congealed into an wide area of strong storms providing mostly heavy rain and small hail. Rainfall amounts ranged from 2 to 5 inches with highest reports of 5.0 inches in Stockton (Winona County), 4.61 inches in the City of Winona (Winona County), and 4.49 inches near Chatfield (Olmsted County). While reports of sub-severe hail were frequent, one storm resulted in ping pong ball sized hail in the Town of Rollingstone (Winona County).","186666":"A series of strong to severe storms traversed western into central Wisconsin from the morning hours of October 24th into October 25th, producing heavy rain and mostly small hail. Rainfall amounts ranged from 2 to 4 inches with highest reports of 6.23 inches in Black River Falls (Jackson County), 5.71 inches near Black River Falls (Jackson County), 5.30 inches in Ettrick (Trempealeau County), and 5.20 inches near Centerville (Trempealeau County). The increased rainfall resulted in flooding over roadways in west-central Wisconsin during the early morning hours of October 25th. A rapid response along the Black River resulted in major to moderate flooding from October 25th through the 27th. The Yellow River in Necedah (Juneau County) also flooded from October 28th into October 30th. Besides heavy rainfall, small hail reports frequented the area, with two severe ping pong sized hail reports recorded in the Town of Trempealeau (Trempealeau County) and Neillsville (Clark County).","186702":"A strong low-level jet and subtle mid-level wave supported isolated convective development, with up to 1000 J/kg of elevated CAPE across northwest Iowa. The strongest cells produced some hail up to quarter size.","186703":"Strong elevated instability near a stalled frontal boundary allowed for a weak impulse to spark an isolated severe thunderstorm across southwest Minnesota, which produced a track of sporadic large hail.","186739":"A couple rounds of showers and thunderstorms resulted in rainfall accumulations ranging from a couple of inches to near 5.5 inches in southern Kansas. This did result in flooding in the typical lower lying areas across the Wichita metro.","186792":"A jet max punched over the northern Great Lakes on 10/24, helping to support multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms through the day. As the first round of storms moved east across the area through the morning, favorable warm and moist air advection helped spark a second round of storms along the existing outflow boundary from the first round. These west-east oriented storms tracked east over Leelanau and Grand Traverse counties, dropping a quick 1-2 inches of rainfall in about an hour overtop of previously saturated ground. This locally heavy rainfall resulted in flash flooding in Traverse City.","186798":"A line of thunderstorms formed over Lake Michigan along a remnant outflow boundary during the late morning of 10/24. These storms eventually moved east over the nearshore waters of Lake Michigan, producing wind gusts in excess of 34 knots.","186829":"A low pressure system off the coast of Vancouver Island pushed fronts throughout Western Washington, bringing lowland rain and first significant mountain snow throughout the area. A couple of record breaking lows were recorded along with maximum daily rainfall."},"events":[{"begin":"2023-10-24T04:55Z","county":"Clay","deaths":0,"end":"2023-10-24T04:57Z","endLat":43.14,"endLon":-95.14,"episode":186702,"id":1145763,"injuries":0,"lat":43.14,"location":"0.86 SE Spencer","lon":-95.14,"magnitude":1.0,"source":"Fire Department/Rescue","state":"IA","type":"Hail","wfo":"FSD"},{"begin":"2023-10-24T05:07Z","county":"Clay","deaths":0,"end":"2023-10-24T05:09Z","endLat":43.15,"endLon":-95.13,"episode":186702,"id":1145764,"injuries":0,"lat":43.15,"location":"1.01 E Spencer","lon":-95.13,"magnitude":1.0,"source":"Social Media","state":"IA","type":"Hail","wfo":"FSD"},{"begin":"2023-10-24T05:59Z","county":"Steele","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2